  <h2 id="what-goes-in"><a class="anchor" href="#what-goes-in">#</a>What goes in</h2>
  <p>Six kinds of input, none of them made for this: floor plans, interior listing photos, street-level exteriors, aerial and drone shots, measured geometry from the city (footprint polygon and LiDAR heights), and the records and listing text.</p>

  <h2 id="fitting-the-plan"><a class="anchor" href="#fitting-the-plan">#</a>Fitting the plan to the footprint</h2>
  <p class="broken-out">This section is now its own note: <a href="/notes/plan-fit/">Fitting a floor plan to the footprint</a></p>
  <p>A drawn floor plan has the right rooms but no position in the world. The city footprint has the right outline but no rooms. Scaling, rotating and anchoring one onto the other got the outlines to 0.85 to 0.93 overlap per level.</p>

  <h2 id="what-comes-out"><a class="anchor" href="#what-comes-out">#</a>What comes out</h2>
  <p>A model you can do two things with. <a class="stub" href="#">See it new</a>: keep the walls and restyle the rooms. <a class="stub" href="#">Change it</a>: move the walls, and get a scope a contractor can price.</p>
